/* ============================================================================
reverse-a-number.epher -- the digits of n read backwards

Builds the reversed number with a packed while loop (guide 1.30): each
pass takes the last digit with mod(n, 10), appends it to the reversed
value by shifting that value left one place (times 10), and drops the
digit with floor(n / 10). The classic check: 12345 reverses to 54321.
Trailing zeros of n become leading zeros of the reversal and vanish,
so 1000 reverses to 1, not 0001.

Source: standard number theory; reversal is the machinery behind
palindromic numbers, the subject of the next script. Exact for every
integer here.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/reverse-a-number.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\integer-sequences\reverse-a-number.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/reverse-a-number.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

There is no knob: the sample numbers are the point of the script.
============================================================================ */

// ---- reverse 12345 ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {remaining digits, reversed so far}; each pass moves the last
// digit onto the end of the reversed value.
v = {12345, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 10), v[2] * 10 + mod(v[1], 10)}
print("reverse of 12345:", v[2])            // 54321

// ---- a few more -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The same loop shape for other values.
v = {2026, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 10), v[2] * 10 + mod(v[1], 10)}
print("reverse of 2026:", v[2])             // 6202
v = {1000, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 10), v[2] * 10 + mod(v[1], 10)}
print("reverse of 1000:", v[2])             // 1: the zeros fall away
v = {12321, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 10), v[2] * 10 + mod(v[1], 10)}
print("reverse of 12321:", v[2])            // itself: a palindrome
/* ---- expected output ----
= {12345, 0}
= reverse of 12345: 54321
= {2026, 0}
= reverse of 2026: 6202
= {1000, 0}
= reverse of 1000: 1
= {12321, 0}
= reverse of 12321: 12321
*/
